CHAR16 must be exactly 16 bits wide.

UEFI defines CHAR16 as a 16-bit unit, and gnu-efi types it as
wchar_t. That is 32 bits on Linux targets, so every string the
bootloader hands to firmware would be laid out wrong. Type it as
unsigned short instead.

The riscv64 hunk is a DIFFERENT fix that rides along in the same file.
inc/efidef.h already typedefs BOOLEAN unconditionally, and riscv64's
efibind.h typedefs it again behind "#ifndef BOOLEAN" -- a guard that
cannot work, since #ifndef tests macros and BOOLEAN is a typedef. The
two conflicting typedefs collide. riscv64 is also the only arch whose
efibind.h lacks CHAR8. Replacing the duplicate BOOLEAN with the
missing CHAR8 fixes both at once, which is what the riscv64 port
(d53caaac4) meant by "definition confusion".

What that hunk does NOT do is give riscv64 the CHAR16 fix above: the
substitution it came from matched one space, and riscv64 writes the
line padded. Nothing compiles the riscv64 header today -- this package
is x86-only and riscv64 has no EFI -- so it is left as it was. See
docs/plans/gnuefi-arch-asymmetry.md.

--- a/inc/ia32/efibind.h
+++ b/inc/ia32/efibind.h
@@ -87,7 +87,7 @@
 
 #include <stddef.h>
 
-typedef wchar_t CHAR16;
+typedef unsigned short CHAR16;
 #define WCHAR CHAR16
 
 
--- a/inc/x86_64/efibind.h
+++ b/inc/x86_64/efibind.h
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@
 
 #include <stddef.h>
 
-typedef wchar_t CHAR16;
+typedef unsigned short CHAR16;
 #define WCHAR CHAR16
 
 typedef uint64_t   UINT64;
--- a/inc/riscv64/efibind.h
+++ b/inc/riscv64/efibind.h
@@ -35,7 +35,7 @@
 typedef wchar_t                 CHAR16;
 #define WCHAR                   CHAR16
 #ifndef BOOLEAN
-typedef uint8_t                 BOOLEAN;
+typedef char       CHAR8;
 #endif
 #undef VOID
 typedef void                    VOID;
